**Read:** [Cryogenic Engineering — M. D. Atrey, IIT Bombay](../sources/Cryogenic-Engineering-Atrey.lingua#r_65F71B6VYE8297NF8ZN1D3S783), gas properties, liquefaction and separation; [Adsorption Science and Technology — Sourav Mondal, IIT Kharagpur](../sources/Adsorption-Science-and-Technology-Mondal.lingua#r_4P0F5BH9XEAJQPGJ0CTTJ76XM5), equilibrium and transport; [Follow oxygen, nitrogen and other air-derived products through separation and use](../../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/inorganic/Air-separation-oxygen-nitrogen-and-gas-specification.lingua#r_2WT6DXK7CD9M2VQ1Q8JSC49PS4) and [Inorganic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Inorganic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_77Z8T5RFYFNNXZ7XKZFKPRKDKR).

Separate the useful molecule's origin from its final delivery state. Air gases require separation of a mixture. Hydrogen can require chemical conversion followed by separation. Carbon dioxide may be recovered from another process, while helium and rare gases depend on particular source streams and recovery opportunities.

Compare product amount, purity, recovery, pressure and phase together. Increasing recovery can carry additional impurities or energy demand. A route making several products needs an account of their simultaneous demand and destination. A purified laboratory sample and a continuous industrial supply pose different scale and continuity questions.

**Work:** Draw an origin-to-product account for nitrogen, hydrogen and helium. Identify which operations create a species, separate it, change its phase or merely transfer it. Write a constituent balance around each major step and locate the first missing process datum.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
